Z||000000||loafer87gt||04-11-2001||12:29 AM||||Found this in TechWeb News <P>The University of North Carolina has finally found a network server that, although missing for four years, hasn't missed a packet in all that time. Try as they might, university administrators couldn't find the server. Working with Novell Inc. (stock: NOVL), IT workers tracked it down by meticulously following cable until they literally ran into a wall. The server had been mistakenly sealed behind drywall by maintenance workers. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||04-11-2001||01:48 PM||||"Novell Tech Support. How can we help you?"<BR>"We've lost one of our servers."<BR>"Do you have the drive backed up?"<BR>"No. We've <I>really lost</I> one of our servers."<P>I passed the first post on to one of my friends. (A server administrator.)<BR>He said that this would never have happened with an NT server. <BR><I>Those</I> have to be rebooted every week.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000002||JSocha||04-11-2001||04:51 PM||||Was it a NOVELL Server or did Novell just help?<P>And if it was a NOVELL Server, wonderin what version of NOVELL Netware they was runnin (v3.xx, 4.xx, ?.xx) cause our two NOVELL Servers (running v4.1x) always do core hexadecimal dumps on me when I least expect it even with all the SP's that have been applied.<P>Thank goodness for backups and for them only being used for file storage.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero5a_3.jpg"> ||||reg||9
